  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist.\n\n**Data findings** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, 14,074 documents, 2026-09-27 to 2026-11-26)\n- `response` is a string holding `200`, `404`, and `503`. There are 441 5xx responses, all 503.\n- Other useful fields: `bytes`, `url.keyword`, `extension.keyword`, `geo.dest`, `clientip`, and `timestamp` (the time field).\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-a00fe4c1 Web logs overview\"**\n- ID: `eff-a00fe4c1-web-logs-overview-16be263b11ed`\n- Time range: 2026-09-01 to 2026-12-31, so it covers all the sample data. I chose this because the data is not near today's date (2026-10-10).\n- Panels:\n  1. Total requests (metric, count)\n  2. Average response size in bytes (metric)\n  3. Requests over time (line, date histogram)\n  4. Requests by response code (pie)\n  5. Top URLs by requests (table)\n  6. 5xx responses (ES|QL metric, `response` 500\u2013599)\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-a00fe4c1 5xx spike\"**\n- ID: `934e5fad-7b97-492c-9bfd-cc2f3b61c8a1`\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, checked every 1m.\n- Counts documents with `response.keyword` from 500 to 599 in the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Tagged `EFF-a00fe4c1`. I enabled it after creating it, and its status is now enabled.\n\n**Things to know**\n- The rule has no actions attached. No connectors exist in this Kibana, so it will change state but will not notify anyone.\n- The 5xx filter uses a string range on `response.keyword`, which is correct for three-digit codes. I did not run the rule to confirm it fires.\n- The sample data is dated in the future relative to today, so the rule's 5-minute window will likely find nothing right now.",
